      <div class="faq-questions">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-item__question">
            Understanding UV Window Film Technology
            <svg class="faq-item__icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
              <path d="M19 13H13V19H11V13H5V11H11V5H13V11H19V13Z" fill="currentColor"/>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="faq-item__answer">
            <p>UV protection window film is a micro-thin polyester layer applied directly to glass surfaces. Modern films use multi-layer technology to reject up to 99% of ultraviolet radiation while maintaining exceptional optical clarity. The film's composition includes UV absorbers embedded within the layers, creating a barrier that prevents both UVA and UVB rays from penetrating.</p>
            <p>In Philadelphia's climate, where humidity and temperature fluctuations create unique challenges for historic properties, quality UV film provides consistent protection year-round. The technology has advanced significantly since early implementations, with current films offering improved longevity, clarity, and performance across all seasons.</p>
            <p>Professional-grade UV films differ substantially from consumer alternatives. Manufacturers like 3M, LLumar, and Vista develop films specifically engineered for the demanding conditions of the mid-Atlantic region, including resistance to Philadelphia's variable humidity levels and temperature extremes.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-item__question">
            Benefits of UV Protection for Philadelphia Properties
            <svg class="faq-item__icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
              <path d="M19 13H13V19H11V13H5V11H11V5H13V11H19V13Z" fill="currentColor"/>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="faq-item__answer">
            <p>Philadelphia's distinctive architecture, from Federal-style rowhouses in Society Hill to Victorian homes in Chestnut Hill, features the large windows that define our city's character. These same windows create ongoing exposure to ultraviolet radiation that causes cumulative damage invisible until it's already occurred.</p>
            <p>The financial impact of UV damage in Philadelphia properties is substantial. Hardwood floors in Center City apartments can fade noticeably within two to three years without protection. Antique furniture in Main Line estates represents irreplaceable value that UV film safeguards indefinitely. Art collections, whether in private homes or commercial galleries throughout Old City, face constant risk from window exposure.</p>
            <p>Beyond property protection, UV window film addresses health concerns increasingly recognized by Philadelphia dermatologists. Daily exposure through home and office windows contributes significantly to cumulative UV exposure, a primary factor in skin aging and skin cancer risk. Properties with large window areas, particularly those facing south or west, benefit most from UV protection.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-item__question">
            Types of UV Window Films We Offer
            <svg class="faq-item__icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
              <path d="M19 13H13V19H11V13H5V11H11V5H13V11H19V13Z" fill="currentColor"/>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="faq-item__answer">
            <p>We install premium UV protection films from the industry's leading manufacturers, each offering distinct advantages for Philadelphia properties. 3M's Prestige series uses a multi-layer optical film technology that provides exceptional UV rejection without metals that can interfere with signals or aesthetics. LLumar's FormulaOne films offer outstanding performance with extensive color and darkness options. Vista professional films combine UV protection with advanced heat rejection for comprehensive window performance.</p>
            <p>For historic Philadelphia properties, we often recommend virtually invisible clear UV films that preserve original glass appearance while providing maximum protection. These films are particularly appropriate for properties with architectural restrictions, period-accurate windows, or homeowners seeking protection without visible changes to their windows.</p>
            <p>Commercial properties throughout Philadelphia's business districts benefit from UV film's ability to protect employees, customers, and merchandise. Retail spaces in Rittenhouse, University City, and Fishtown can display merchandise near windows without UV damage concerns. Office buildings throughout Center City reduce employee exposure while protecting equipment and furnishings from sun-related deterioration.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-item__question">
            Performance Metrics & Specifications
            <svg class="faq-item__icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
              <path d="M19 13H13V19H11V13H5V11H11V5H13V11H19V13Z" fill="currentColor"/>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="faq-item__answer">
            <p>Professional UV window film performance is measured across multiple standards recognized by the National Fenestration Rating Council (NFRC) and tested according to American Society for Testing and Materials (ASTM) specifications. Key metrics include Ultraviolet Light Rejection (UVR), which measures the percentage of UV radiation blocked; Visible Light Transmission (VLT), indicating how much natural light passes through; and Solar Heat Gain Coefficient (SHGC), measuring heat rejection capability.</p>
            <p>Quality UV protection films typically achieve 99% or greater UV rejection while maintaining 70-80% visible light transmission in clear configurations. Tinted variants offer increased heat rejection with proportionally reduced light transmission. All films we install carry independent laboratory certification and manufacturer warranties backing their performance claims.</p>
            <p>The NFRC label on installed film provides Philadelphia property owners with verified performance data comparable across manufacturers. This standardization allows informed decisions based on actual performance rather than marketing claims, essential for historic preservation projects requiring documented protection levels.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-item__question">
            Applications Across Philadelphia
            <svg class="faq-item__icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
              <path d="M19 13H13V19H11V13H5V11H11V5H13V11H19V13Z" fill="currentColor"/>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="faq-item__answer">
            <p>Residential applications for UV protection window film span Philadelphia's diverse housing stock. In Center City high-rises, floor-to-ceiling windows maximize views and natural light but also maximize UV exposure. Townhouses throughout South Philadelphia, Northern Liberties, and Passyunk benefit from UV film on south-facing windows that receive intense afternoon sun. Properties in Manayunk, Roxborough, and the river wards experience unique exposure patterns based on their hillside positions.</p>
            <p>Historic properties in Rittenhouse Square, Chestnut Hill, and Society Hill present specific challenges where UV protection must balance preservation needs with aesthetic requirements. Original windows in these properties often cannot be replaced, making window film the ideal solution for adding UV protection without altering irreplaceable historic glass.</p>
            <p>Commercial applications throughout the Delaware Valley include office buildings in Conshohocken and King of Prussia, retail spaces along Chestnut and Walnut Streets, healthcare facilities throughout University City, and institutional properties serving Philadelphia's educational and cultural communities. Each application receives customized analysis addressing the specific UV exposure patterns of that building's orientation and use.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-item__question">
            Our Professional Installation Process
            <svg class="faq-item__icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
              <path d="M19 13H13V19H11V13H5V11H11V5H13V11H19V13Z" fill="currentColor"/>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="faq-item__answer">
            <p>Our installation process begins with a comprehensive consultation at your Philadelphia property. We assess window orientations, exposure levels, existing conditions, and your specific protection priorities. This evaluation determines optimal film selections and identifies any preparatory work needed for older or historic windows.</p>
            <p>Professional installation by our Philadelphia-based crews typically completes residential projects in a single day. Commercial installations schedule according to building operations, often completing work in evenings or weekends to avoid business interruption. Our installers are manufacturer-certified and experienced with the unique challenges of historic Philadelphia window configurations.</p>
            <p>Post-installation, we conduct thorough quality inspection and provide complete documentation of installed products, performance specifications, and warranty registration. Our relationship with Philadelphia clients continues through periodic check-ins and maintenance guidance, ensuring long-term satisfaction with your UV protection investment.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-item__question">
            Warranty, Maintenance & Lifespan
            <svg class="faq-item__icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
              <path d="M19 13H13V19H11V13H5V11H11V5H13V11H19V13Z" fill="currentColor"/>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="faq-item__answer">
            <p>Professional UV window film installations include comprehensive manufacturer warranties typically ranging from 10 years to lifetime coverage for residential applications. These warranties cover film degradation, bubbling, peeling, and delamination — the primary failure modes for properly installed quality film. Commercial installations receive correspondingly robust warranty coverage tailored to business use patterns.</p>
            <p>Maintenance requirements for UV protection film are minimal. Regular cleaning with soft cloths and appropriate cleaning solutions maintains optical clarity. Unlike window replacements, film does not require ongoing adjustment, weather-stripping, or specialized maintenance throughout its service life. This reliability makes film particularly suitable for rental properties and commercial buildings where maintenance resources are limited.</p>
            <p>Quality UV films maintain their performance characteristics for 15-20 years in Philadelphia's climate, with many installations continuing to perform well beyond twenty years. The long lifespan means most Philadelphia property owners will rarely need to consider re-installation, making UV film a permanent, low-maintenance solution for ongoing sun protection.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-item__question">
            Cost & Return on Investment
            <svg class="faq-item__icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
              <path d="M19 13H13V19H11V13H5V11H11V5H13V11H19V13Z" fill="currentColor"/>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="faq-item__answer">
            <p>UV protection window film installation in the Philadelphia area typically costs $8-15 per square foot depending on film selection, window configuration complexity, and installation conditions. Specialty films for historic properties or demanding performance requirements may cost more, while standard configurations offer excellent protection at competitive rates. We provide detailed quotes following in-person assessments, ensuring accurate pricing for your specific requirements.</p>
            <p>Return on investment for UV window film manifests through multiple channels. Faded floor replacement in Philadelphia averages $8-15 per square foot for quality hardwood — comparable to film costs but representing a one-time fix versus ongoing damage without protection. HVAC cost reduction from heat rejection provides monthly savings, particularly significant during Philadelphia's humid summers when air conditioning runs continuously in heavily windowed spaces.</p>
            <p>For Philadelphia investment properties, UV film protects asset values by maintaining interior conditions that attract and retain quality tenants. Commercial properties throughout the region benefit similarly, with protected interiors maintaining rental values and tenant satisfaction. Most property owners recognize complete payback within three to five years through combined savings on potential damage, energy costs, and asset preservation.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
